95
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
In this week's podcast.

96
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
well, we're going to talk about a familiar subject.

97
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
They came up again this week

98
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,000
on a conversation that I had with a scouter friend.

99
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Honestly, I don't think it's something

100
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
that we can talk about too much,

101
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
but we'll be talking about leadership positions,

102
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
positions of responsibility

103
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
and how to evaluate them,

104
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
And then we have several great email questions

105
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
that came in over the past week.

106
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
That's going to take up the rest of the podcast.

107
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,000
So let's get started. shall we

108
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Scout MasterChip? in seven minutes

109
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
Or less,

110
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
Let's go.

111
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
I was having an online conversation

112
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
with a scouter friend

113
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
and he said that when a scout goes

114
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
for a scout master conference

115
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
or border review,

116
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
there's nothing that can necessarily be done

117
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
to prevent that scout

118
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
from claiming the fulfillment

119
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
of a leadership position,

120
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
even if they never did anything for it,

121
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,000
And that he was trying to create

122
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
standards, specific for their troop,

123
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,000
that will still meet the BSA

124
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
expectations but aren't so rigid

125
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
that they scare scouts away

126
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,000
from leadership positions

127
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
or prevent good scouts

128
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
who are growing and showing effort

129
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
but don't meet all the standards

130
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
from getting credit.

131
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
And this sounds like a great idea, doesn't it?

132
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,000
A lot of scouts reach the point

133
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
where they're a little frustrated

134
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
with their ability to inspire scouts

135
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
towards responsibility and leadership.

136
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
They see themselves

137
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
evaluating positions

138
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
of responsibility-

139
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,000
We're going to talk about that in depth here-

140
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
But they see themselves

141
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
trying to evaluate this

142
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
and they don't really have any standards

143
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
or any written things

144
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
to hand and they feel like

145
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
it's a very difficult process

146
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
and that there are scouts

147
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,000
who don't do anything

148
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
in a leadership position

149
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
of responsibility other than wearing a patch,

150
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
and they feel like

151
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
this is unfair.

152
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
I want to unpack the whole subject once again

153
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
and talk about it once again,

154
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
because it is something that comes up frequently.

155
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
Now, you can't

156
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
and shouldn't

157
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,000
create a set of standards for this.

158
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,000
Now think about this.

159
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,000
If a set of standards that are written down

160
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
somewhere, some metrics,

161
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
that kind of pin all this down-

162
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
would be useful,

163
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
Somebody would have come up with these

164
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
and they would be printed in the Scoutmaster Handbook-

165
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
Right.

166
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
But you're not going to find anything like that

167
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,000
in an entire century of

168
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
Scoutmaster Handbooks.

169
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,000
We don't need them.

170
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
We don't want to address the question

171
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
with that kind of an answer.

172
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
I want to further say it's impossible

173
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
for a scout to get to the end

174
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
of the tenure in his position

175
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
of responsibility

176
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
and for there to be any question

177
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
if they have fulfilled that tenure,

178
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
if the Scoutmaster

179
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
is doing their job.

180
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
We need to talk about an important distinction

181
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
because,

182
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
in the shorthand that we develop as Scouters

183
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
we call

184
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
the Senior Patrol Leader,

185
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
Patrol Leader, Quartermaster, Scribe

186
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
and all the other positions that are defined

187
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
in the Scout Handbook

188
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
to be leadership positions.

189
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
But if you look carefully

190
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
you'll see that all the requirements

191
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
in our life in Eagle

192
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
do not call these leadership positions at all.

193
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
They call them positions

194
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
of responsibility.

195
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
One of the other perennial questions is:

196
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
is how does somebody like the Scribe

197
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
or the Quartermaster demonstrate leadership?

198
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Well, not every position

199
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
that a Scout occupies in a troop

200
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:35,000
is necessarily a leadership position.

201
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
Some of them

202
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
are required that a Scout be

203
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
responsible

204
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
for what needs to be done

205
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
to get the job complete correct.

206
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
So there's an important distinction here.

207
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
It's important to look at these positions

208
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
for what they are:

209
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
Positions of responsibility.

210
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
So what we are evaluating

211
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
principally

212
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
is not some kind of fuzzy

213
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
leadership standard.

214
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
We are looking at

215
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
the level of responsibility

216
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
that Scouts

217
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
progress in,

218
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
both through the tenure in one of those positions.

219
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
Now, does that make sense?

220
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
It should be a reasonably simple thing to do.

221
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
It's the level of

222
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
progress

223
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
in responsibility

224
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
that Scouts achieve

225
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
in the tenure of that position.

226
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
Now I want to go back to the idea that

227
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
it's impossible

228
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
to get to a Scoutmaster conference

229
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
or a border review

230
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
and have there be any question

231
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
as to whether these things were fulfilled.

232
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
if the Scoutmaster

233
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
is doing their job-

234
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
And what I mean by that is

235
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
I talk to each one of my Scouts

236
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
holding a position of responsibility

237
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
several times a month.

238
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
I know what they're doing.

239
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
If there are any concerns about

240
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
how they're doing the job,

241
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
we talk about them.

242
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
We don't wait six months later

243
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
at a Scoutmasters conference for me to announce:

244
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
well, I've been watching you do this job

245
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
and I don't think you're doing it very well,

246
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
so I'm not going to count this

247
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
towards your advancement.

248
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
That's unfair.

249
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
I make sure that Scouts know

250
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
what is expected of them,

251
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
largely by asking them to tell me

252
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
what they think is expected of them.

253
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
When they tell me what the job

254
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
that they're taking on involves

255
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
and what they feel constitutes success

256
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
in that position,

257
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
then we have a shared understanding

258
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
of what's going on.

259
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
I may add to what they think

260
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
they may have, but not usually.

261
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
They usually get it,

262
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
And then I'm very conscious

263
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
of keeping up with them.

264
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
If they don't show up, it's my job

265
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
to find out why. If things are going

266
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
badly, I talk to the Scout

267
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
and I encourage them to do better.

268
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
Again, by asking them questions

269
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
about what's going on, I get them

270
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
to explain to me what they are doing,

271
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
or perhaps what they're not doing,

272
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
